('0e667365-9b3a-5bff-b312-35bbc131a763'::uuid,'91559d3e-3452-546c-ad4f-8b66c64cd08a'::uuid,'v1.0 introduction',E'![Manual of Operations cover](/moo-cover.webp)\n\n**INTRODUCTION**\n\nORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO)\nAND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)\n\n*Assessment-informed working draft\nSubject to institutional, operational, technical and legal validation*\n\n**Draft 1.0 | 12 November 2026**\n\n**CONTROLLED WORKING DRAFT**\n\n# DOCUMENT CONTROL\n\n| **Document attribute** | **Control information** |\n|------------------------|-------------------------|\n| Document title         | Ormoc City CDRRMO–EOC Manual of Operations – Introduction |\n| Chapter title          | Introduction |\n| Document status        | Working draft for technical, institutional and legal validation |\n| Document owner         | City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office |\n| Technical custodian    | CDRRMO Head |\n| Reviewing offices      | City Legal Office; OCD Region VIII; City Planning and Development Office; City Budget Office; DILG City LGOO |\n| Version / date         | Draft 1.0 / 12 November 2026 |\n| Approval authority     | To be determined under the City document-control and approval arrangements |\n| Review cycle           | At least annually; after a significant emergency, exercise, material legal or technical change, or identified operational deficiency |\n\n## REVISION AND APPROVAL RECORD\n\n| **Version** | **Date** | **Description** | **Prepared / reviewed by** | **Approval** |\n|-------------|----------|-----------------|----------------------------|--------------|\n| 1.0 | 12 Nov 2026 | Initial introduction: purpose, how the manual is organized (ten-chapter roadmap, five Parts, ten functional areas, four DRRM pillars), the assessment method stated once, document control and citation policy, and the manual''s relationship to the MODE platform. | Project drafting team | For review |\n\n# 1. Purpose of this Manual\n\nThis Manual of Operations (MOO) tells the City Government of Ormoc **who does what, when, on what authority, and to what standard** in disaster risk reduction and management (DRRM), before, during and after emergencies. It is the single controlled reference for the City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office (CDRRMO) and the Emergency Operations Center (EOC).\n\nThe manual exists to make the CDRRMO–EOC institution:\n\n- **Compliant** — every procedure traces to a legal or policy authority (RA 10121 and its IRR, RA 12287 and its IRR, NDRRMC issuances, and City ordinances and executive orders), and mandatory (M) indicators are separated from good-practice (GP) indicators so the City always knows what it *must* do and what it *should* do.\n- **Context-specific** — written for Ormoc''s actual hazards (typhoon, riverine and flash flood along the Anilao–Malbasag corridor, rain-induced landslide, storm surge, earthquake) and its **85 barangays** (PSA PSGC figure adopted by plebiscite on 8 October 2022; the City master list is being reconciled with CPDO, the City Secretary and the DILG City LGOO before the number is treated as final).\n- **Efficient** — built for a small office: the full institutional assessment takes about **five field days with a two-to-three person team**, and a short priority set of indicators carries most of the readiness gain (see §4.4).\n- **Evidence-based** — nothing is claimed without proof (see §4).\n- **Actionable** — every gap found by the assessment converts into a costed, owned, dated action in the manual''s action plan.\n\n# 2. How this Manual is Organized\n\n## 2.1 The ten-chapter roadmap\n\nThe full manual is compiled into **ten chapters**. Chapters 1–5 carry the drafted text to date; the remaining chapters are drafted next, drawing their requirements from the functional-area outlines in the MODE platform Library.\n\n| **Ch.** | **Title** | **Status (12 Nov 2026)** |\n|---------|-----------|--------------------------|\n| 0 | Introduction | Drafted (v1.0) — this chapter |\n| 1 | Legal Mandate, DRRM Governance and Institutional Accountability | Drafted (v1.2) |\n| 2 | CDRRMO Organizational Structure, Staffing and Human Resource Management | Drafted (v1.2) |\n| 3 | Risk Intelligence, DRRM and Contingency Planning, and Institutional Preparedness | Drafted (v1.1) |\n| 4 | Early Warning, EOC Activation, Incident Management and Emergency Response | Drafted (v1.1) |\n| 5 | Recovery, Rehabilitation and Build Back Better | To be drafted |\n| 6 | DRRM Financing, Resource Management and Accountability | To be drafted — a financing and procurement working draft exists and will be carried into this chapter at compilation |\n| 7 | Monitoring, Evaluation, Knowledge Management and Continuous Improvement | To be drafted |\n| 8 | Stakeholder Engagement and Institutional Partnerships | To be drafted |\n| 9 | Public Awareness, Community Resilience and Capacity Development | To be drafted |\n| 10 | Institutional Sustainability, Deep Resilience and Recognition | To be drafted |\n\nThe chapter order follows the life of an institution: its legal foundation (Ch. 1), its people (Ch. 2), its plans and risk knowledge (Ch. 3), its operations when hazards strike (Ch. 4), the return to normal and better (Ch. 5), the money and resources that make all of this possible (Ch. 6), the learning loop (Ch. 7), the external relationships that extend the City''s reach (Ch. 8), the resilience of communities themselves (Ch. 9), and the long-term sustainability and excellence agenda (Ch. 10).\n\nChapter numbers are assigned at compilation. Drafts keep their working numbers until the City adopts the full manual; where a working draft carries a different number (for example, the financing draft seeded as Chapter 5), it is renumbered without changing its content.\n\n## 2.2 Parts, functional areas and the four DRRM pillars\n\nUntil all ten chapters are drafted, the operational spine of the manual is the **ten functional areas** of the MODE platform Library, grouped into **five Parts** (ADR-012):\n\n| **Part** | **Functional areas** |\n|----------|----------------------|\n| 1. Legal mandate and governance | 1 Governance, Organization & Institutional Arrangements |\n| 2. SOPs by alert level | 3 Preparedness, Planning & Capacity Development; 4 EOC Activation, Incident Management & Coordination; 7 Resource, Logistics & Emergency Support Management; 8 Responder Safety, Welfare & Inclusive Emergency Operations |\n| 3. Information management and public communication | 2 Risk Intelligence, Prevention & Mitigation; 5 Early Warning, Emergency Communications & Public Information; 6 Situational Awareness, Information Management & GIS |\n| 4. Continuity and infrastructure safeguards | 9 Digital Continuity, Cybersecurity & Systems Resilience |\n| 5. Recovery and continuous improvement | 10 Recovery, Learning, Performance & Continuous Improvement |\n\nEach functional area belongs to exactly one of the **four DRRM thematic pillars**, which color-code the Library and the rest of the platform:\n\n- **Prevention and mitigation** — areas 2 (Risk Intelligence, Prevention & Mitigation) and 9 (Digital Continuity, Cybersecurity & Systems Resilience)\n- **Preparedness** — areas 1 (Governance, Organization & Institutional Arrangements) and 3 (Preparedness, Planning & Capacity Development)\n- **Response** — areas 4 (EOC Activation, Incident Management & Coordination), 5 (Early Warning, Emergency Communications & Public Information), 6 (Situational Awareness, Information Management & GIS), 7 (Resource, Logistics & Emergency Support Management) and 8 (Responder Safety, Welfare & Inclusive Emergency Operations)\n- **Recovery** — area 10 (Recovery, Learning, Performance & Continuous Improvement)\n\nEvery chapter, SOP, protocol, workflow, form and annex names the functional area it serves, so a reader can always move between the chapter view (how the manual reads) and the area view (how the institution works).\n\n# 3. Document Control and Citation Policy\n\n- The **approved manual is the legal document**. The MODE platform makes it easy to read, search and use; where the platform and the approved manual differ, the approved manual governs.\n- Versions move through **draft → in review → approved**, and only the **CDRRMO Head** approves a version for use. Approved versions are immutable; changes come as new versions.\n- Every SOP cites its authority in a control block. Where a rule is read from a **scanned or unofficial copy** of an issuance (for example, NDRRMC Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018), the reading is **provisional** until verified with OCD Region VIII and the City Legal Office, and the manual says so on the page. Parameters the law leaves open are stored as adjustable rules, not hard-coded guesses.\n- Citations on the platform render with the note: *the official text governs.*\n\n# 4. How We Assess Readiness: the Institutional Assessment Tool (IAT)\n\n**This section is stated once, here. No chapter repeats it; chapters apply it.**\n\n## 4.1 What the IAT measures\n\nThe **Institutional Assessment Tool (IAT) v1.7** measures the capacity of the CDRRMO (Part A), the EOC (Part B), and the CDRRMO–EOC interface (Part C):\n\n- **140 indicators** in **22 domains** — Part A: 82 indicators in 10 domains; Part B: 43 indicators in 7 domains; Part C: 15 interface indicators in 5 domains.\n- Each indicator is typed **M (mandatory)** or **GP (good practice)** and weighted 1–3 by criticality. Part C indicators have no M/GP type: they score the quality of the hand-off between the office and the center.\n- Every indicator maps to at least one **master requirement** (MR-01 to MR-18) in the Traceability and Data Dictionary, and to the manual sections, SOPs and forms that close its gap.\n\n## 4.2 Scoring rules (evidence-gated)\n\nEach indicator scores **0–4**: 0 = not present; 1 = initial; 2 = developing; 3 = established; 4 = advanced.\n\n- A score of **3 or 4 requires documentary proof** — the plan, EO, ordinance, MOA, log, report or form must exist and be shown.\n- **Interviews and workshops alone can never score above 2.** What people say is a lead, not evidence.\n- Blank barangay counts stay blank; a count that is not reported is not zero.\n\n## 4.3 From scores to action\n\nAssessment results feed, in one pass:\n\n- the **maturity index** per domain and part;\n- the **Institutional Maturity Index (IMI)** for the CDRRMO and EOC;\n- the **Interface Index** (Part C) for the CDRRMO–EOC hand-off;\n- **compliance rates** for M indicators (what the law requires) and GP indicators (what good practice expects);\n- the **gap priority** ranking; and\n- the **action plan** — each gap becomes an action with an owner office, a target quarter and a status, reviewed every cycle.\n\n## 4.4 The priority set (Pareto)\n\nA small set of indicators carries most of the readiness gain. The platform''s **Today dashboard** tracks this set live:\n\n- **Tier 1:** A1.1, A1.2, A2.2, A2.3, A4.1, A4.2 — legal mandate, council reconstitution, staffing and fund-access basics.\n- **Tier 2:** B1.1, B1.2, B1.3, A3.3, A6.1, A6.2, A6.3, B4.1, B4.2, A8.6, A8.8 — EOC establishment, alert levels, contingency planning, warning thresholds and anticipatory action.\n- **Tier 3:** A4.6, A10.4, A10.6, A10.8, A7.3, A1.8 — audit, M&E, recovery procedures and the State of Imminent Disaster workflow.\n\nA Tier 1 indicator is never left at 2 if the City can raise it to 3 with one ordinance, one executive order or one funded staffing item.\n\n# 5. The Manual and the MODE Platform\n\n**MODE** — the *Manual of Operations for the DRRMO and EOC* — is the mobile-first, offline-capable platform that hosts this manual and runs the institution around it:\n\n- **Assess** (IAT) — score the 140 indicators with the evidence gates of §4.2; results roll up to maturity indices and the action plan.\n- **Library** — this manual: chapters, annexes, SOPs, workflows and forms, with citations and the RefText legal catalogue.\n- **Protocols and Workflows** (DRRM) — the point-of-action checklists and multi-office cycles that carry out the SOPs during events and duty shifts.\n- **EOC** — activation levels, the EOC board, situational reports and anticipatory action under RA 12287.\n- **Tools** — the ICS simulator, the continuity (PSCP) workbench and the Gawad KALASAG self-check; all keep their data in the browser.\n\nThe platform follows **privacy by schema**: it never stores the names of evacuees, affected persons or informants — barangay reports are counts, consistent with the Data Privacy Act (RA 10173). Ten platform roles, enforced in the database, mirror the accountabilities in this manual.\n\n# 6. Reading Guide\n\n- **New to the manual?** Read this Introduction, then Chapter 1 (what the law requires) and Chapter 4 (what happens on event day).\n- **Looking for a procedure?** Search the Library by functional area or pillar color, or open the SOP registry — every protocol and form names the SOP it carries out and the indicators it closes.\n- **Validating a chapter?** Use the revision record at its head, check each SOP''s control block against its cited authority, and record the validation outcome in the platform''s Library workflow.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch0_Introduction_v1.0.md (Introduction, v1.0 introduction)','draft'),
('1fc69b3a-095b-54f9-9c9d-ea638e9a2f33'::uuid,'91559d3e-3452-546c-ad4f-8b66c64cd08a'::uuid,'v1.1 official working draft (assessment-informed method and systems diagnosis)',E'**MANUAL OF OPERATIONS**\n\nINTRODUCTION TO THE MANUAL\n\n**ORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO)**\n\n**AND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)**\n\n*Purpose, legal basis, method, design principles and conventions for all ten chapters*\n\n\n**Draft 1.1 \\| 12 November 2026**\n\n\n# 1. Purpose of the Manual\n\nThis Manual of Operations (MOO) is one integrated manual for the City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office (CDRRMO) and the Emergency Operations Center (EOC) of the City Government of Ormoc. It sets out who decides, who acts, in what order, with which records and under which law, across normal operations, preparedness, early warning, anticipatory action, emergency response, recovery and continuous improvement.\n\nThe Manual is produced through a fourteen-week co-creation between the City Government of Ormoc and its consultant, Alvin M. Silva (ASilva Innovations). It is assessment-informed: each chapter answers gaps found by the Institutional Assessment Tool (IAT) and names the indicators it addresses. It is also operational: each standard operating procedure (SOP) is written so that it can be run as a checklist in the MODE platform and tested in an exercise.\n\nThree limits apply to the whole Manual:\n\n-   **It is not a source of legal power.** The Manual implements authority that already exists in law, ordinance and valid executive issuance. Where an authority is missing, the Manual proposes the instrument (for example, an EOC establishment EO) and labels it as a proposal.\n\n-   **Writing a procedure is not evidence of using it.** A procedure counts as institutionalised only when it is approved, used, recorded and reviewed. The IAT scores reflect this rule.\n\n-   **Every chapter remains a controlled working draft** until the owning office validates it and the CDRRMO Head approves it. Seeded content in MODE is never marked approved by the drafting team.\n\n# 2. The City and Its Risk Context\n\nOrmoc is an independent component city in the Province of Leyte, Eastern Visayas (Region VIII). Under the Philippine Standard Geographic Code (PSGC) the City (code 0803738000) has **85 barangays as of 31 July 2025**. The count follows City Ordinance No. 052, s. 2021 and the plebiscite of 8 October 2022, which merged the 28 numbered Poblacion districts into Barangays South (14), East (7) and West (7) and renamed District 29 as North (110 − 28 + 3 = 85). Plans prepared before the merger, including the CLUP 2017–2027 and parts of the CDRA, still use 110 barangays; chapters apply a 110-to-85 crosswalk to those sources. The CPDO, the City Secretary and the DILG City LGOO have still to sign off the City master list, so 85 is the working figure in controlled records.\n\nThe City is exposed to tropical cyclones and storm surge, riverine floods and flash floods (notably along the Anilao and Malbasag rivers), rain-induced and earthquake-induced landslides, earthquakes and liquefaction, tsunami, drought and El Niño, epidemics (including dengue), and industrial and geothermal incidents. Short-lead hazards such as flash floods and landslides usually fall below the lead time needed for a State of Imminent Disaster and are handled through city-level early action (Chapter 4). The 1991 flash flood remains the deadliest event on record; Super Typhoon Yolanda (2013), the magnitude 6.5 Leyte earthquake (2017) and Typhoon Odette (2021) shaped the current arrangements (Chapter 5, Table 5.3).\n\n**Table I.3**\n\n*Principal Offices for DRRM Governance in Ormoc (2025–2028 term)*\n\n| **Office**                                                                                                 | **Role in the Manual**                                                                                                                                     |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **City Mayor (Lucy Torres-Gomez, 2025–2028)**                                                              | Local chief executive; chair of the CDRRMC; issues executive orders, including any State of Imminent Disaster EO.                                          |\n| **Vice Mayor and Sangguniang Panlungsod (presiding officer: Vice Mayor Leo Carmelo "Toto" L. Locsin Jr.)** | Enacts ordinances and appropriations; declares a State of Calamity on CDRRMC recommendation; approves the LDRRMF investment plan and the recovery program. |\n| **City DRRM Council (CDRRMC)**                                                                             | Statutory policy, coordination and oversight body, reorganised by EO No. 2024-032A.                                                                        |\n| **CDRRMO (Head: Ciriaco C. Tolibao)**                                                                      | Permanent technical and operational office, created by Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 as amended by Ordinance No. 011, s. 2015; secretariat of the CDRRMC.     |\n| **Emergency Operations Center**                                                                            | Activated coordination mechanism, housed with the CDRRMO OpCen; not a fourth CDRRMO division and not a field command.                                      |\n\n*Note.* Names are given for orientation only; the Manual assigns duties to positions, never to persons. The CDRRMO Head''s name is to be confirmed with HRMO before printing, as City sources show different middle initials.\n\n# 3. Legal and Policy Basis\n\nChapter 1, section 4, sets out the complete legal and policy framework, including the register of local issuances. Table I.4 lists the instruments that every chapter relies on and the facts about them that the Manual uses consistently. Each citation in the Manual and in MODE ends with the rule that the official text governs.\n\n**Table I.4**\n\n*Core Instruments and the Facts the Manual Uses Consistently*\n\n| **Instrument**                                                                                 | **What the Manual relies on**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **RA No. 10121 (2010) and IRR**                                                                | CDRRMC (Sec. 11), CDRRMO (Sec. 12), State of Calamity (Sec. 16), LDRRMF of at least 5% of estimated revenue from regular sources, split 30% Quick Response Fund and 70% for prevention, mitigation, preparedness, response, rehabilitation and recovery (Sec. 21).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| **RA No. 12287 (2025), Declaration of State of Imminent Disaster Act, and its IRR**            | Signed into law on 12 September 2025. The IRR was signed on 14 September 2026; its effectivity depends on publication and filing with the UP Office of the National Administrative Register. The lead-time rule is **three (3) days, extendable to five (5)**. AA funds may use up to 10% of the 70% LDRRMF component. Disbursement tranches (up to 70% within a three-day lead time; at most 30% beyond it) apply to hydrometeorological and geological hazards only. A declaration lifts automatically when the hazard occurs. Rule IV has ten sections and gives no count of actions; the MODE checklist derived from it is labelled as MODE-derived. |\n| **NDRRMC Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018 (25 October 2018)**                                       | EOC establishment, activation triggers, five management functions, the rule that the EOC and the incident command post are not co-located, and LGU EOC funding chargeable to the LDRRMF. It lets each EOC set its own alert codes. It is a Memorandum, not a Memorandum Circular.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| **National Disaster Response Plan, as restated in RDRRMC VIII Resolution No. 12-006, s. 2023** | Source of the White, Blue and Red alert levels: White, normal operations; Blue, at least 50% of resources on standby; Red, all resources available.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| **DILG VIII Memorandum R08-LGCDD-2025-02-26-008 (26 February 2025)**                           | Harmonised disaster response reporting template and SOP: pre-disaster, during, post-disaster and terminal reports; LGU-to-Province cut-offs 6:00 AM and 6:00 PM at White, 9:00 AM and 7:00 PM at Blue and Red.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| **JMC No. 2014-1; JMC No. 2013-1; DBM LBC 124**                                                | LDRRMO structure and plantilla; LDRRMF programming and use.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| **COA Circular No. 2012-002**                                                                  | LDRRMF and Special Trust Fund accounting. The section on trust-fund transfers is cited inconsistently in secondary sources (4.4 or 5.1.10) and must be verified against the circular before it is quoted.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| **RA No. 10173; NPC Circular 16-03; NPC Circular 2023-06**                                     | Privacy by design; 72-hour breach notification (16-03, still in force); security measures (2023-06).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| **NDRRMC MC No. 3, s. 2025; OCD Memorandum No. 763, s. 2026 (corrected)**                      | Gawad KALASAG CY 2026: Gold 2.50 and above, Silver 2.00–2.49, Bronze 1.50–1.99.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| **Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 and No. 011, s. 2015; EO No. 2024-032A**                          | Local creation of the CDRRMO and current CDRRMC composition (Chapter 1).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n\n> VALIDATION NOTE. Legal readings taken from scanned copies remain at medium confidence until checked against certified copies by the City Legal Office and OCD Region VIII. Where a reading is contested, MODE keeps the value as a rule parameter with its status, and an ADR records whose reading it is.\n\n# 4. Structure of the Manual\n\nThe Manual has ten chapters, adopted on 12 November 2026. Chapters 1 to 3 lay the foundations: law and governance, the organisation and its people, and the risk knowledge and plans that preparedness rests on. Chapters 4 to 6 follow the operations cycle: warning and response, recovery, and the money and resources that pay for both. Chapters 7 to 9 make the system learn and reach outward: Chapter 8 deals with external relationships and partnerships, and Chapter 9 with building resilience in people, communities and institutions. Chapter 10 draws on all of them for long-term sustainability and excellence (Figure I.1).\n\n**Figure I.1**\n\n*Architecture of the Ten-Chapter Manual of Operations*\n\n![Figure 1: 4. Structure of the Manual](/library/introv11/fig-01.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this Introduction from the chapter sequence adopted on 12 November 2026.\n\n**Table I.5**\n\n*Chapters, Assessment Anchors, Platform Home and Status*\n\n| **Ch.** | **Title**                                                                 | **Main IAT anchors**               | **MODE home**                                 | **Status (12 Nov 2026)**                      |\n|---------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| 1       | Legal Mandate, DRRM Governance and Institutional Accountability           | A1, A10.5, B1.1, Part C governance | Library, Vault                                | Draft 1.3 (this revision)                     |\n| 2       | CDRRMO Organizational Structure, Staffing and Human Resource Management   | A2, B4, B2 (facility)              | EOC module, roster, settings and roles        | Draft 1.3 (this revision)                     |\n| 3       | Risk Intelligence, Planning and Institutional Preparedness                | A3, A5                             | Library, workflows WF-SOP3-01 to 09           | Draft 1.2 (this revision)                     |\n| 4       | Early Warning, EOC Activation, Incident Management and Emergency Response | A6, A7, A8, B1.2–B1.5, B5          | EOC and AA engine, SitRep forms, protocols    | Draft 1.2 (this revision)                     |\n| 5       | Recovery, Rehabilitation and Build Back Better                            | A9, A8.7, A10.6                    | Workflows and protocols (proposed)            | Draft 1.0 (12 Nov 2026)                       |\n| 6       | DRRM Financing, Resource Management and Accountability                    | A4, A7.5                           | WF-BUDGET-CYCLE, AA fund calculator, Vault    | Drafted as "Chapter 5" v1.0; to be renumbered |\n| 7       | Monitoring, Evaluation, Knowledge Management and Continuous Improvement   | A10.3–A10.9, A5.4                  | IAT and analytics, action-plan feed           | To draft                                      |\n| 8       | Stakeholder Engagement and Institutional Partnerships                     | A10.1–A10.2, Part C                | Part C crosswalk, liaison pages, EOC clusters | To draft                                      |\n| 9       | Public Awareness, Community Resilience and Capacity Development           | A6.4–A6.6, A7, A2.6                | Training records, tools                       | To draft                                      |\n| 10      | Institutional Sustainability, Deep Resilience and Recognition             | A10.7, B3.3, B3.5                  | Public pages, GK self-check, Vault            | To draft                                      |\n\n*Note.* Anchors follow Part III of the Coherence Analysis (12 November 2026). Contractual functional modules, the ten functional areas (MR-02 to MR-11) and the chapter sequence are different levels of the same Manual, not competing structures.\n\n# 5. Assessment-informed Method and Systems Diagnosis\n\n## 5.1 The Institutional Assessment Tool\n\nThe Manual rests on the Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool (IAT), version 1.7 (workbook v1-7, 11 November 2026). It has 140 indicators in 22 domains: Part A, CDRRMO, 82 indicators in 10 domains; Part B, EOC, 43 indicators in 7 domains; and Part C, interface with other City offices and partners, 15 indicators in 5 domains. Parts A and B have 79 Mandatory (M) and 46 Good Practice (GP) indicators and form the Institutional Maturity Index (IMI). Part C has no Mandatory items, names a lead office other than the CDRRMO, and is reported as a separate Interface Index that is never added to the IMI.\n\n**Table I.6**\n\n*Scoring Rubric, Evidence Rule and Index Bands*\n\n| **Element**                         | **Rule**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n|-------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Rubric**                          | 0 Absent; 1 Initial (verbal only); 2 Developing (draft or partial); 3 Compliant (approved document and proof of use); 4 Advanced (level 3 plus a test, exercise or after-action review showing improvement); NA with written justification. |\n| **Evidence rule (Methodology 5.2)** | A score resting only on focus group discussion or interviews is capped at 2. Until validation, the lower score stands.                                                                                                                      |\n| **Index**                           | Sum of score × weight divided by the sum of weights of scored items. Weights: 3 life-safety or legal; 2 effectiveness; 1 supportive.                                                                                                        |\n| **Bands**                           | Below 0.5 Absent; 0.5–1.49 Initial; 1.5–2.49 Developing; 2.5–3.49 Compliant; 3.5 and above Advanced. The unrounded value is banded.                                                                                                         |\n| **Mandatory compliance**            | Scored Mandatory items at 3 or 4, divided by scored Mandatory items. A domain is "Compliant" only at 100%.                                                                                                                                  |\n| **Gap priority**                    | (4 − score) × weight × 1.5 for Mandatory items, × 1.0 for Good Practice and all Part C items. High 9 and above; Medium 4 to 8.9; Low below 4.                                                                                               |\n\n## 5.2 Methods and Evidence\n\nThe October–November 2026 assessment used four methods. Informants are identified by office only, per RA 10173.\n\n**Table I.7**\n\n*Assessment Methods Used in October–November 2026*\n\n| **Method**                      | **Coverage**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       | **Main use**                                                                         |\n|---------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Focus group discussion**      | CDRRMO Head and staff at the CDRRM Office, which houses the OpCen and the EOC; indicators scored together on the IAT workbook.                                                                                                                     | Baseline scores, later capped by the evidence rule where no document supported them. |\n| **Key informant interviews**    | CDRRMO Chiefs of Operations, Research and Planning, and Administration; CPDO; CSWDO; City Administrator''s Office.                                                                                                                                  | Roles, interfaces and decision rights.                                               |\n| **Observation and site visits** | EOC; Operations and Rescue Office; Barangay Liloan (evacuation site, protocol and camp management).                                                                                                                                                | EOC status, duty system, evacuation and camp practice.                               |\n| **Document review**             | DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032, CDP 2024–2030, LCCAP 2019–2030, CDRA, CLUP 2017–2027; City issuances 2016–2025 (ordinances, executive orders, CDRRMC and SP resolutions, registered in the MODE Vault); CDRRMO organisational chart and staff papers. | Legal basis, Council practice, declarations and funding decisions.                   |\n\n## 5.3 Baseline Results\n\nWith the evidence rule applied, the baseline Institutional Maturity Index is **1.98 (Developing)**: 2.06 for the CDRRMO (Part A) and 1.85 for the EOC (Part B). Mandatory compliance is **32.9%** (26 of 79 Mandatory items at 3 or 4). The Interface Index for Part C is 1.41 (Initial). Figure I.2 shows the domain indices. All rows remain "Pending validation" until the 1–4 December 2026 workshop.\n\n**Figure I.2**\n\n*Domain Maturity Indices, IAT v1.7 Baseline*\n\n![Figure 2: 5.3 Baseline Results](/library/introv11/fig-02.webp)\n\n*Note.* Computed from the A_CDRRMO and B_EOC sheets of IAT v1.7 with the workbook formula. Dashed lines are the band thresholds. A9 (recovery), B4 (EOC organisation and staffing), B5 (information management) and B6 (coordination and resources) are the weakest domains.\n\n> VALIDATION NOTE. The City''s statement of design principles describes "108 indicators in 17 domains", while the controlled workbook (v1-7) has 140 indicators in 22 domains. Until the City decides which instrument is controlled, the Manual uses the workbook in the repository (140/22). The 108-indicator Google Sheet is IAT v1.0, which the Vault records as superseded.\n\n## 5.4 Systems Diagnosis of the CDRRMO and EOC\n\nThe baseline scores describe what is present or missing. A systems reading of the same evidence explains why the gaps persist and where a change in the Manual will have the largest effect. This section applies the iceberg model, causal loop diagrams and system archetypes to the IAT v1.7 results. It uses only the controlled figures in Table I.8; it is an interpretation for validation, not an additional score.\n\n**Table I.8**\n\n*Baseline Figures Used in the Systems Diagnosis*\n\n| **Measure (IAT v1.7)**                           | **Value**                      | **Reading**                                                |\n|--------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Institutional Maturity Index (Parts A and B)** | 1.98                           | Developing                                                 |\n| **Part A, CDRRMO / Part B, EOC**                 | 2.06 / 1.85                    | Both Developing; the EOC trails the office                 |\n| **Mandatory compliance**                         | 32.9% (26 of 79)               | Two in three legal or life-safety items not yet at level 3 |\n| **A1 Legal Mandate and Governance**              | 2.47; 4 of 6 M items compliant | Strongest governance base                                  |\n| **B1 EOC Legal Basis and ConOps**                | 2.23; 1 of 5 M items compliant | EOC rests on practice, not on an issuance                  |\n| **Weakest domains**                              | B4 1.31; A9 1.38; B6 1.38      | EOC staffing, recovery, coordination and resources         |\n| **Interface Index (Part C)**                     | 1.41                           | Initial; reported separately, never added to the IMI       |\n| **High-priority gaps**                           | 18 Part A, 12 Part B, 2 Part C | Gap priority ≥ 9                                           |\n\n*Note.* Computed from IAT v1.7 with the workbook formulas and the evidence rule (FGD-only scores capped at 2). Earlier figures (IMI 2.13; Mandatory compliance 41.8%) came from v1-5 and v1-6 before the evidence rule was applied and are superseded.\n\n### The Iceberg Model\n\nThe iceberg model separates what people see during an event from the patterns, structures and mental models below it (Figure I.3). The visible events are familiar: activation decided case by case, Council resolutions adopted for each event, staff stretched across OpCen, field and EOC duties, and late or uneven situation reports. Below the waterline, the evidence points to four structures.\n\n-   The CDRRMO is an office under the Office of the City Mayor, created by Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 (amended by Ordinance No. 011, s. 2015) with a Head (SG-23) and three LDRRMO I posts. Most of its workforce (about 47 staff, of whom four are permanent, as reported at the FGD) is on job-order or casual terms (A1.1 and A2.3, each scored 2).\n\n-   No executive order establishes the EOC, its levels, its activation authority or its staffing (B1.1, scored 2).\n\n-   The boundary between incident command (ICS) and the EOC is not written down, so the same people play both roles (B6.1, scored 1).\n\n-   Standing rules exist, such as Ordinance No. 003, s. 2013 on forced evacuation and Ordinance No. 135, s. 2023, yet decisions are still taken through event-by-event resolutions rather than through procedures that cite them.\n\nThe mental model underneath is that disaster management is response: capability is shown by how hard the office works during an event, not by the rules, rosters and records it keeps between events.\n\n**Figure I.3**\n\n*Iceberg Model of the CDRRMO and EOC Baseline*\n\n![Figure 3: The Iceberg Model](/library/introv11/fig-03.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this Introduction from the IAT v1.7 baseline, the focus group discussion and the register of City issuances. Indicator codes refer to IAT v1.7.\n\n### Causal Loop: Shifting the Burden\n\nFigure I.4 shows the dominant dynamic. Operational pressure from each event is relieved quickly by ad-hoc fixes: borrowed staff, a new resolution, an improvised EOC set-up (balancing loop B1). Fundamental solutions, namely plantilla positions, an EOC executive order and approved SOPs, also relieve the pressure but only after a delay (balancing loop B2). Each successful ad-hoc fix increases reliance on ad-hoc fixes and reduces the attention and resources given to the fundamental solutions (reinforcing loop R1). Over time the office becomes better at improvising and no better at institutionalising.\n\n**Figure I.4**\n\n*Shifting the Burden: Ad-hoc Fixes Versus Fundamental Solutions*\n\n![Figure 4: Causal Loop: Shifting the Burden](/library/introv11/fig-04.webp)\n\n*Note.* Causal loop diagram. "+" means the variables move in the same direction; "−" means opposite directions; the double bar marks a delay. B = balancing loop; R = reinforcing loop.\n\n### Causal Loop: ICS–EOC Mandate Conflation\n\nFigure I.5 shows a second, reinforcing loop (R2). Without a codified boundary, the EOC is drawn into field command; the more it commands, the less it coordinates information and resources; incidents then need more direct intervention, which pulls the EOC further into command. The loop is broken by writing the boundary down: the Incident Commander keeps incident-level command, and the EOC supports coordination, information, resources and executive decisions (SOP 4-05; indicator B6.1).\n\n**Figure I.5**\n\n*ICS–EOC Mandate Conflation and the Codified Boundary*\n\n![Figure 5: Causal Loop: ICS–EOC Mandate Conflation](/library/introv11/fig-05.webp)\n\n*Note.* Causal loop diagram. The codified boundary (SOP 4-05, B6.1) is the intervention that weakens loop R2.\n\n### System Archetypes\n\n**Table I.9**\n\n*System Archetypes Observed in the Baseline*\n\n| **Archetype**                  | **Evidence in the baseline**                                                                                                                                                                      | **Response in the Manual**                                                                                                                                                          |\n|--------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Shifting the Burden**        | Event-by-event resolutions and borrowed staff relieve pressure; plantilla, the EOC executive order and approved SOPs remain drafts (A1.1, A2.3, B1.1).                                            | Amend Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 (Annex 1-A); EOC executive order (Annex 1-C); SOPs that cite standing ordinances (Chapter 4).                                                      |\n| **Growth and Underinvestment** | Mandates and expectations grow (RA 12287, Gawad KALASAG, new ordinances on CESU, volunteers and BHERTs) while staffing, the EOC facility and information systems lag (B4 1.31, B5 1.47, B6 1.38). | Staffing plan and three-deep EOC roster (Chapter 2); EOC information management and reporting (Chapter 4); financing linked to the gaps (Chapter 6).                                |\n| **Drifting Goals**             | With 32.9% Mandatory compliance, the working standard risks settling at "we responded" rather than "we met the standard".                                                                         | Fixed standards: level 3 needs an approved document and proof of use; Mandatory gaps tracked to closure through the corrective action register (Chapter 1, section 9.5; Chapter 7). |\n\n*Note.* Archetypes after Senge (1990) and Meadows (2008), applied to IAT v1.7 evidence. These are interpretations for the validation workshop.\n\n## 5.5 Leverage Points and Master Requirements\n\nMeadows (1999) ranks places to intervene in a system from weak (parameters) to strong (paradigms). The Manual works at the stronger levels: it changes rules, boundaries, information flows and feedback, not only budgets and numbers. Table I.10 links each level to the Manual, the master requirements (MR-01 to MR-18 in the Traceability Data Dictionary v1.2) and the MODE mechanism that keeps the change in place. The 18 master requirements trace to the 140 IAT indicators; they are not separate indicators.\n\n**Table I.10**\n\n*Leverage Points, Manual Actions and Master Requirements*\n\n| **Leverage level**           | **System element**                                                                                            | **Manual action**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | **Master req.**                         | **MODE mechanism**                                                      |\n|------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Paradigm and goals**       | From "disaster management is response" to "risk is governed through approved, tested and improved procedures" | One integrated Manual; Build Back Better (Chapter 5); TQM and PDCA (Chapter 1, section 9; Chapter 7)                                                                                                                                                  | MR-01, MR-11, MR-18                     | Manual versioning; Sendai and Ten Essentials crosswalk                  |\n| **Rules**                    | Legal basis of the office and the EOC                                                                         | Amendment of Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 (Annex 1-A); EOC executive order (Annex 1-C); Part C lead-office rules                                                                                                                                        | MR-02, MR-15, MR-17                     | Vault register of issuances; role-based access enforced in the database |\n| **Boundaries and structure** | ICS–EOC boundary; resource authority                                                                          | SOP 4-05; resource requests tracked as requested, approved, mobilised, delivered and demobilised                                                                                                                                                      | MR-05, MR-08                            | EOC resource request workflow; locked decisions                         |\n| **Information flows**        | Warning, decision and anticipatory-action information                                                         | SOP GOV-05 (Annex 1-E) for RA 12287: lead time of three days, extendable to five; tranches up to 70% within three days and up to 30% beyond, hydrometeorological and geological hazards only. Warning-action matrix citing City ordinances (SOP 4-02) | MR-03, MR-06, MR-07, MR-16              | Anticipatory-action rules table; logbook; SitRep templates              |\n| **Feedback and controls**    | Accountability, evidence and correction                                                                       | RACI in every chapter; corrective action register (Chapter 1, section 9.5; Chapter 7); evidence gate                                                                                                                                                  | MR-12, MR-13, MR-14 (with MR-09, MR-10) | Scoring engine in SQL; evidence cap; v_traceability view                |\n\n*Note.* Leverage levels after Meadows (1999). Master requirements from the Master Requirements Traceability and 140-Indicator Data Dictionary v1.2. RA 12287 parameters as stated in its IRR (signed 14 September 2026); the official text governs.\n\n> VALIDATION NOTE. The systems diagnosis interprets baseline evidence that is still "Pending validation". If the December 2026 workshop changes indicator scores, the figures in this section and the order of leverage points will be updated in the next version of this Introduction.\n\n# 6. Design Principles\n\nThe City set five design principles for the assessment and the Manual. Table I.11 states what each demands and how the chapters meet it.\n\n**Table I.11**\n\n*Design Principles and Their Application Across the Manual*\n\n| **Principle**        | **What it demands**                                                                            | **How the Manual applies it**                                                                                                                                             |\n|----------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Compliant**        | Every requirement cites its legal or policy source; Mandatory is separated from Good Practice. | Each SOP control block names its policy basis and legal decision authority; indicator tables show M or GP.                                                                |\n| **Context-specific** | Ormoc''s hazards, barangays, offices and language.                                              | 85 PSGC barangays; local issuances register (Chapter 1); short-lead hazards routed to city-level early action; plain language suitable for Cebuano and Waray translation. |\n| **Efficient**        | An instrument sized for the field; one form closes each step.                                  | Every protocol or workflow step closes on a named form; registers are reused across chapters.                                                                             |\n| **Evidence-based**   | A score of 3 or 4 needs documentary or observed proof.                                         | Validation notes mark provisional statements; records of use are listed for every SOP.                                                                                    |\n| **Actionable**       | Scores convert to prioritised gaps and an action plan.                                         | Each chapter has an implementation priorities table traced to the IAT Action Plan; the Pareto priorities in section 9 order the work.                                     |\n\n# 7. Systems Thinking, Quality Management and Coherence\n\nThe Manual treats disaster risk as the result of interconnected hazards, exposure, vulnerability, capacities and dependencies. Its offices, councils and procedures are therefore written as one system with feedback loops rather than as separate units. Total Quality Management supplies the operating discipline: every process has an owner; decisions rest on evidence; procedures are standardised and measured; and corrective action closes the Plan–Do–Check–Act cycle. Each chapter states only the systems and quality principles that are specific to its subject; the general approach is set here once.\n\n**Figure I.6**\n\n*How the Assessment, the Manual and the MODE Platform Work Together*\n\n![Figure 6: 7. Systems Thinking, Quality Management and Coherence](/library/introv11/fig-06.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this Introduction from the MODE system design (docs/system-design.md, section 1).\n\nTo keep the Manual, the IAT and the platform coherent, every statement a user sees must pass five tests:\n\n**Table I.12**\n\n*The Five Coherence Tests*\n\n| **Test**      | **Meaning**                                                                                                                                                                 |\n|---------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Traceable** | It links to a master requirement (MR-01 to MR-18), IAT indicators and a legal provision.                                                                                    |\n| **Cited**     | Its source exists and says what the statement says.                                                                                                                         |\n| **Singular**  | One barangay denominator (85), one indicator set, one source for alert colours and one set of Gawad KALASAG tiers, with no parallel versions in chapters, seeds or screens. |\n| **Enforced**  | In MODE, the rule sits in the database with access controls; screens mirror it and are tested against it.                                                                   |\n| **Versioned** | Approved versions are never edited; corrections are made through a superseding version or assessment cycle.                                                                 |\n\n# 8. Institutional Invariants\n\nThe following rules hold in every chapter. A chapter may add detail but may not contradict them.\n\n1.  The CDRRMC is the statutory policy, coordination and oversight body; the CDRRMO is the permanent technical and operational office and the CDRRMC secretariat.\n\n2.  The EOC is an activated coordination mechanism. It is not a fourth CDRRMO division and not a field command. The Incident Commander and the incident management team keep incident-level command; the EOC supports coordination, information, resources and executive decisions.\n\n3.  EOC activation does not declare a State of Calamity (RA 10121, by the Sanggunian on CDRRMC recommendation) or a State of Imminent Disaster (RA 12287, by the Mayor''s EO on the prescribed recommendation). The three paths stay distinct.\n\n4.  Sector offices, agencies, utilities and barangays keep their own mandates. A RACI allocates work; it does not transfer legal authority.\n\n5.  The Manual creates no independent power to order evacuation, declare no-build zones, spend funds or procure. It follows the authority in law and valid City issuances.\n\n6.  Rainfall, wind, flood, tsunami and landslide thresholds are never invented. They come from the official products of PAGASA, PHIVOLCS and MGB and from City-approved contingency plans, with their uncertainty recorded.\n\n7.  Duties are assigned to positions, never to persons.\n\n8.  No general-purpose record holds the names of evacuees, patients or informants. Counts are aggregates by barangay, a blank means "not reported", never zero, and counts are scoped to the current event.\n\n# 9. Priority Indicators\n\nNot every gap has the same leverage. An indicator is a priority when it passes three tests: it cascades (achieving it lifts other indicators), the CDRRMO controls it, and it can move within one budget or EOC cycle. Table I.13 lists the "vital few" by IAT v1.7 identifier. At the December 2026 workshop they are presented in tier order, Mandatory compliance first.\n\n**Table I.13**\n\n*Priority Indicators by Tier*\n\n| **Tier**                     | **Indicators**                                           | **Why they come first**                                                                                                                                                                        |\n|------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1\\. Legal constitution       | A1.1; A1.2; A2.2 and A2.3; A4.1 and A4.2                 | CDRRMO created as a regular office; CDRRMC complete; permanent Head and minimum JMC 2014-1 positions; LDRRMF at 5% with the 70/30 split. Without these, most other indicators cannot score.    |\n| 2\\. Operating systems        | B1.1–B1.3; A3.3; A6.1–A6.3; B4.1 and B4.2; A8.6 and A8.8 | EOC established by EO with levels and activation procedures; hazard-specific contingency plans; working early warning; EOC structure staffed two deep; PDRA and anticipatory-action protocols. |\n| 3\\. High-leverage compliance | A4.6; A10.4; A10.6; A10.8; A7.3; A1.8                    | AA funding pathway; LDRRMP M&E framework; after-action reviews; the 45-day post-lifting report; BDRRMC functionality across the 85 barangays; State of Imminent Disaster procedure.            |\n\n*Note.* From Part II, section II.3 of the Coherence Analysis. The workbook will carry a priority flag for these identifiers so that the Action Plan ranks gaps by leverage, control and immediacy rather than by deficit alone.\n\n# 10. The Manual and the MODE Platform\n\nMODE, the Manual of Operations for the DRRMO and EOC Development Platform, supports the Manual but is not the Manual. The approved Manual remains the legal document; MODE holds its current text, version history and the evidence that it is used. MODE does four jobs: it **assesses** the office and the EOC with the IAT; it **writes and traces** the Manual, linking every section to its indicators and master requirements; it **runs** SOPs as protocols and workflows with owners, timings and an audit trail; and it **operates** the EOC and the RA 12287 anticipatory-action process.\n\n**Table I.14**\n\n*MODE Design Rules That the Manual Relies On*\n\n| **Rule**                          | **What it means for users of the Manual**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n|-----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **City ownership**                | The City owns the code and the data. The production database is a City Supabase project in Tokyo (ap-northeast-1); the privacy policy names the transfer.                                                                                                                     |\n| **Local-first**                   | Field staff can capture logbook entries, forms and barangay figures without a connection; they sync when the device is back online.                                                                                                                                           |\n| **Authority actions online only** | Changing the EOC level, approvals, declaration steps, disbursements and role grants need a connection and are never queued.                                                                                                                                                   |\n| **Ten roles**                     | CDRRMO Head, EOC Manager, EOC staff, duty officer, assessor, validator, lead office focal person, author, viewer and admin, enforced in the database. Only the CDRRMO Head approves, retires or reopens Manual versions and protocols; a draft protocol runs only as a drill. |\n| **Immutability**                  | Approved versions, validated responses, approved SitReps, decided resource requests, declarations and disbursements are locked. The logbook is append-only; corrections are new entries. Vault documents are superseded, never deleted.                                       |\n| **Vocabulary (ADR-019)**          | **SOP** is controlled Manual text. A **protocol** is a point-of-action checklist. A **workflow** is a multi-office management cycle. A **form** is the record that closes a step. A template is never called an SOP.                                                          |\n| **Citations**                     | Every cited law or guideline opens a short note that ends "The official text governs."                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n\n*Note.* Summarised from docs/system-design.md and docs/design-system.md in the MODE repository (version 1.16.0).\n\n# 11. Document Conventions\n\n## 11.1 Standard Chapter Template\n\nEvery chapter follows the same order so that readers find the same things in the same place: cover; document control; revision and approval record; introduction and assessment findings for the chapter; purpose and intended results; scope, application and institutional boundaries; legal, policy and operational framework (chapter-specific instruments only; the general framework is here and in Chapter 1); chapter-specific systems and quality principles; the operational sections; standard operating procedures with control blocks and step tables; RACI; performance monitoring and corrective action; implementation priorities; forms, registers and annexes; relationship with other chapters; conclusion; references; validation and source control register; annexes.\n\n## 11.2 Numbering, Captions and Notes\n\n**Table I.15**\n\n*Numbering and Presentation Conventions*\n\n| **Element**                  | **Convention**                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n|------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Tables and figures**       | Numbered by chapter (Table 4.12, Figure 5.3; Table I.4 in this Introduction). APA style: the number in bold on one line, the title in italic title case below it, and a "Note." under the table or figure giving its source. |\n| **SOPs**                     | SOP chapter-sequence with two digits (SOP 3-06, SOP 4-01, SOP 5-07). Chapters 1 and 2 keep their thematic series (GOV-01 to GOV-08; HR-01 to HR-08), which MODE already uses.                                                |\n| **SOP step tables**          | Each step states its actor, action, decision rule, inputs, outputs and records, timing, control and escalation.                                                                                                              |\n| **Forms and annexes**        | Forms carry a chapter or thematic prefix (EWR-F01, REC-F06); where MODE already had a form, its MODE code is kept and both codes are shown. Annexes are lettered by chapter (Annex 4-C).                                     |\n| **Validation notes**         | Boxed text beginning "VALIDATION NOTE." marks a statement that is provisional or needs a named reviewer. Each one is also listed in the chapter''s validation and source control register.                                    |\n| **Dates and times**          | Day, month, year ("12 November 2026"; "15 Oct 2026, 14:05" in tables), Manila time.                                                                                                                                          |\n| **Barangay counts and data** | PSGC barangays (85); aggregates only; blank means not reported.                                                                                                                                                              |\n\n## 11.3 Typography and Colour\n\nThe chapters use the three type families of the MODE design system, with Roboto added for table text: chapter titles in Montserrat Bold (the standard, "classic" cut; there is no separate font named Montserrat Classic); subtitles and section titles in Poppins Bold; subsection titles in Roboto Bold; body text in Roboto Condensed with single line spacing; table text in Roboto 10 pt. Colours follow the design-system tokens: navy (#0F3D66) for titles and figure outlines, light blue (#EEF5FB) and gray-blue (#E3EBF2) for fills, and orange (#D66812) only to mark what is active or to draw attention, never to show a status. The White, Blue and Red level colours are used only where an EOC level is meant.\n\n# 12. Pending National Issuances\n\nSeveral instruments that will bind the City have not yet been issued. They are tracked as parameters with a status, not as settled rules.\n\n**Table I.16**\n\n*National Issuances Pending as of 12 November 2026*\n\n| **Pending issuance**                                                                                                        | **Expected from**                         | **Chapters affected** |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|-----------------------|\n| Operational guidelines on triggers, thresholds and lead times, including drought, under RA 12287                            | NDRRMC                                    | Chapters 1, 3 and 4   |\n| Funding guidelines for anticipatory action                                                                                  | DBM and COA                               | Chapters 4 and 6      |\n| Anticipatory-action monitoring and evaluation framework (due about 13 December 2026)                                        | NDRRMC / OCD                              | Chapters 4 and 7      |\n| Issuance creating the National Anticipatory Action Technical Working Group                                                  | NDRRMC                                    | Chapters 1 and 8      |\n| Emergency procurement for anticipatory action (existing: GPPB Resolution No. 17-2026, Circular No. 08-2026, 14 August 2026) | GPPB (in force); further rules may follow | Chapter 6             |\n\n# 13. Corrections Applied Across Chapters 1 to 4\n\nThis revision applies the factual corrections of the Coherence Analysis to Chapters 1 to 4 so that all chapters state the same facts. Reviewers should check them first.\n\n**Table I.17**\n\n*Corrections Applied in Chapters 1 to 4 (12 November 2026)*\n\n| **Subject**        | **Earlier text**                                                                              | **Text now used**                                                                                                                                   |\n|--------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Chapter structure  | Nine-chapter plan; financing as Chapter 5; recovery as Chapter 8; EOC operations as Chapter 6 | Ten chapters as in Table I.5; every cross-reference updated                                                                                         |\n| Chapter titles     | Ch.3 "…DRRM and Contingency Planning…"; Ch.4 "…Activation…"                                   | Ch.3 "Risk Intelligence, Planning and Institutional Preparedness"; Ch.4 "Early Warning, EOC Activation, Incident Management and Emergency Response" |\n| NDRRMC Memo 131    | "Memorandum Circular No. 131"                                                                 | "Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018"; alert colours attributed to the NDRP as restated in RDRRMC VIII Res. No. 12-006, s. 2023                             |\n| RA 12287 lead time | "72 hours"                                                                                    | "three (3) days, extendable to five (5)"                                                                                                            |\n| IRR of RA 12287    | "released on 29 September 2026"                                                               | signed 14 September 2026; effective on publication and ONAR filing                                                                                  |\n| PSGC reference     | "PSGC (fourth quarter 2022)"                                                                  | "PSA PSGC as of 31 July 2025"                                                                                                                       |\n| Local issuances    | Being compiled; 48 issuances (2021–2024)                                                      | Full register of local DRRM issuances 1993–2025 (Chapter 1, section 4.5 and Annex 1-I)                                                              |\n| SOP numbering      | Ch.3 "SOP 3-1"                                                                                | "SOP 3-01"                                                                                                                                          |\n| Introductions      | General method and approach repeated in each chapter                                          | Stated once in this Introduction; chapters open with their own findings                                                                             |\n\n# 14. Abbreviations\n\n**Table I.18**\n\n*Abbreviations Used Across the Manual*\n\n| **Abbreviation**    | **Meaning**                    